An element is algebraic over $F$ when some nonzero <polynomial> in $F[x]$ vanishes at it. Its <minimal polynomial> is the <monic polynomial> of least positive <degree> with this property; scaling a least-<degree> nonzero vanishing <polynomial> makes it monic.

If $m_1,m_2$ are two such <polynomials>, they have the same least <degree>. Their difference has smaller <degree> because both are monic, and it also vanishes at $a$. Minimality forces the difference to be zero. Hence **the monic <minimal polynomial> is unique**.

= ii
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Divide $f$ by $m$ in the <polynomial ring> over the field: $f=qm+r$, where $r=0$ or $\deg r<\deg m$. If $f(a)=0$, then $r(a)=0$, and minimality forces $r=0$. Conversely a multiple of $m$ vanishes at $a$. Therefore
$$
\boxed{f(a)=0\iff m\mid f.}
$$
This includes the zero <polynomial>.

= iii
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

If $m=uv$ with both factors of positive <degree>, evaluation gives $u(a)v(a)=0$. Since $K$ is a field, one factor vanishes at $a$, contrary to the minimal <degree> of $m$. Thus **$m$ is an <irreducible polynomial> over $F$**.

For the final unnumbered request, $\zeta=e^{\pi i/3}=\frac12+i\frac{\sqrt3}2$ satisfies $\zeta^2-\zeta+1=0$. The monic quadratic $x^2-x+1$ has no rational root, since its discriminant is $-3$. It is therefore an <irreducible polynomial> over $\mathbb Q$, and
$$
\boxed{m_\zeta(x)=x^2-x+1.}
$$

