The <total derivative> is a <linear map> $Df(a):\mathbb R^n\to\mathbb R^n$ such that
$$
\boxed{f(a+h)=f(a)+Df(a)h+r(h),\qquad\frac{\|r(h)\|}{\|h\|}\longrightarrow0\quad(h\to0).}
$$
It is unique: the difference of two such <linear maps>, evaluated on $h=tv$, must vanish on every fixed <vector> $v$ after division by $t$ and passage to the limit.

= ii
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The <directional derivative> in the specified <vector> direction is
$$
\boxed{D_{\mathbf u}f(a)=\lim_{t\to0}\frac{f(a+t\mathbf u)-f(a)}t=Df(a)\mathbf u.}
$$
The equality follows from the total-derivative remainder estimate; for the zero direction the result is zero. The convention keeps the supplied <vector> and does not replace it by a unit <vector>.

= iii
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

The $j$th column of the <matrix> of $Df(a)$ in the standard bases is $Df(a)e_j$. Its $i$th component is $\partial f_i/\partial x_j(a)$, found by taking increments $te_j$. Thus **the <matrix> of the <total derivative> is the <Jacobian matrix>**, with entry $(i,j)$ equal to $\partial f_i/\partial x_j(a)$.

= iv
{parent=1}
