Pearson's statistic is
$$
\boxed{X^2=\sum_{i=1}^4\sum_{j=1}^3\frac{(O_{ij}-E_{ij})^2}{E_{ij}}.}
$$
For <independent> observations under the <independence> <null hypothesis>, its large-sample null distribution is approximately $\chi^2$ with $(4-1)(3-1)=6$ <statistical degrees of freedom>. The fitted row and column probabilities account for the reduction in <statistical degrees of freedom>. All <expected counts> exceed five, so the usual approximation has adequate expected cell sizes here. Reject <independence> when the statistic exceeds the corresponding upper-tail <critical value>.

= iii
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

There are six <statistical degrees of freedom>, so the supplied 1% <critical value> is $q_6=16.81$. Since $42.25>16.81$, **reject <independence> at the 1% <significance level>**. The data give strong evidence of an association between the two consumption classifications in this group of participants.

